course: adult-life
lesson: 12
title: Cars, chores and the shopping list
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Running a home is hundreds of small jobs: washing, cooking, shopping, remembering what has run out, looking after the car and the garden, and somebody has to do every one of them.
explain: A car costs money all the time: fuel, a yearly safety test, repairs, fines if it is parked in the wrong place, and a service to rescue it if it breaks down.
explain: Picture a Saturday in Leeds: the trolley that was meant for "three things" comes out full, there's a yellow packet on the windscreen, and at home Dad shouts "who finished the milk?" while Mum says "I'm not your personal maid!"
explain: Grown-ups grumble about chores because the work never ends and much of it is invisible, so a family that shares the jobs is a calmer family.
fact: In the UK, cars over three years old need a yearly safety test called an MOT, and a car that fails must be repaired before it can be driven legally.
fact: The price of petrol goes up and down with what happens around the world, and when it rises families have less money for other things.
fact: A yellow plastic packet stuck to the windscreen is a parking ticket, a fine that must be paid within a set number of days or it gets bigger.
fact: Breakdown recovery is a service, often paid for yearly, that sends someone to fix a broken-down car or tow it to a garage.
fact: On a motorway, after a breakdown everyone gets out and waits behind the barrier, away from the traffic.
fact: "I'm not your personal maid!" means the person feels they are doing all the tidying for everyone and is fed up.
fact: A chore chart lists the household jobs and who does each one, so work is shared fairly and nobody has to nag.
fact: A shopping list on the fridge or a phone is how families remember what has run out, so whoever finishes something should add it.
fact: Supermarkets are designed to make people buy more than they planned, with offers, smells and treats by the tills.
fact: "Your room looks like a bomb's hit it" is an exaggeration meaning the room is very messy.
fact: Much housework is invisible, like booking appointments and planning meals, so each grown-up may not see what the other does.
fact: Gardens need regular weeding, mowing and watering or they quickly grow wild.
exercise: Dad says "The car's due its MOT". What is an MOT? | A yearly safety test for cars over three years old | A new car the family is buying, with a three-year guarantee | A kind of motorway | A fine for driving too fast
exercise: What happens if a car fails its MOT? | It must be repaired before it can be driven legally | It is taken away to the scrapyard and crushed the same day | Nothing, the driver just tries again next year | The driver has to go to prison
exercise: Mum at the pump: "Have you seen the price of petrol?" What does she mean? | Petrol has got expensive | Petrol has run out everywhere | The pump is showing the wrong time | She wants you to read the sign aloud
exercise: Why does the price of petrol change? | It depends on what happens around the world | The petrol station owner picks a new price for fun each day | It goes up only on Mondays | It changes with the weather at the pump
exercise: You find a yellow plastic packet stuck to the windscreen. What is it? | A parking ticket, a fine | A free gift from the shop | A leaflet about a car wash | A note from a friend
exercise: What happens if a parking fine is not paid in time? | It gets bigger | It is forgotten after a week | The car is given a free MOT | The fine goes to the passengers
exercise: The car breaks down on the motorway. Where should everyone wait? | Out of the car, behind the barrier | Inside the car with the doors locked | On the hard shoulder next to the traffic | In the middle lane to wave cars round
exercise: Dad calls "the recovery people". Who are they? | A service that fixes the car or tows it to a garage | The police who give out fines to drivers who break down | The people who sell new cars | A doctor who checks everyone is well
exercise: Mum shouts "I'm not your personal maid!" What does she mean? | She is fed up of tidying up after everyone | She is applying for a job as a maid in a big hotel in town | She wants someone to hire a maid tomorrow | She has lost her apron
exercise: Why does a chore chart go up on the fridge? | So the jobs are shared fairly and nobody has to nag | So the fridge looks more colourful | So the children can pick only the jobs they like | So visitors know whose turn it is to cook for them
exercise: Dad: "Who used the last of the milk and didn't put it on the list?" What should you do when you finish something? | Add it to the shopping list | Hide the empty carton | Buy a new one yourself with pocket money every time | Tell Gran to bring some
exercise: "We're only going in for three things." Why does the trolley come out full? | Supermarkets are designed to make people buy more | The shop adds extra things to every trolley | Three things always come in big boxes | The list had a hundred things on it
exercise: "Your room looks like a bomb's hit it." What does Mum mean? | Your room is very messy | There was a real explosion upstairs | Your room needs painting | Your room smells of smoke
exercise: Mum: "I do everything around here!" Dad: "No you don't!" Why do they see it so differently? | Lots of housework is invisible, so each misses what the other does | One of them is lying on purpose to win | They are arguing about the children's behaviour at school and at home | Dad really does everything and Mum has forgotten
exercise: Gran says "The weeds won't pull themselves up." What does she mean? | The garden needs work or it will grow wild | Weeds pull themselves up if you wait long enough | She wants to buy some new weeds | The weeds are too strong to ever pull
exercise: What is a good way for a child to help with the shopping? | Hold the list and tick things off | Put treats in the trolley when no one looks | Push the trolley as fast as possible | Choose everything by the tills
test: overheard: "£47 for a few bags?? we came in for bread, milk and eggs!" whats happened | They bought much more than they planned, which shops are designed for | The bread, milk and eggs cost £47 each | The till made a mistake on purpose | They paid for someone else's shopping by mistake at the self-checkout
test: Your brother says "MOT means the car is brand new". Put him right. | No, it is a yearly safety check for older cars | Yes, every new car gets the letters MOT on its plates | No, it means the car has been sold | Yes, MOT is the shop where new cars live
test: "sod it, another ticket, I was there 5 minutes!" Dad shouts, ripping a yellow packet off the glass. What must he do now? | Pay the fine within the time given, or it grows | Throw it away, five minutes doesn't count at all | Stick it on another car | Nothing, tickets are only warnings
test: On the news: "Petrol hits record high." What might families do? | Drive less and spend less on other things | Buy more cars | Leave the engine running all day | Go on more long trips
test: Smoke from the bonnet, hard shoulder, lorries roaring past. Your sister wants to sit in the car to stay warm. What is right? | Get out and wait behind the barrier with the grown-ups | Stay in the car with the seatbelt on and the doors locked | Stand by the car to wave for help | Walk along the motorway to find a garage
test: mum: "right, nobody gets pocket money till that chart's got ticks on it". what's the chart for | Sharing out the household jobs | Counting how many sweets everyone ate | Planning the summer holiday | Keeping score in a board game
test: "who drank the last of the milk AGAIN?? it's not on the list!!" Why is Dad cross? | Nobody wrote it on the list, so nobody bought more | Milk is not allowed in the house | He wanted to put the milk on the list himself, as he always does | The list was lost in the bin
test: Your friend says "my mum said my room's been hit by a bomb, are we on the news?" What is true? | No, it is an exaggeration for a very messy room | Yes, a small bomb fell on the room | Yes, the news reports messy bedrooms | No, it means the room needs new wallpaper
test: Mum: "I booked the dentist, sorted the birthday present, did the school forms..." Dad: "I didn't know you did all that." What does this show? | A lot of housework is invisible | Dad does not like the dentist | Mum is making it all up | School forms are Dad's job only
test: Gran, kneeling in the soil: "Leave it two weeks and it's a jungle out here." What does she mean? | Gardens grow wild quickly unless they are looked after | A real jungle is being planted in her garden by the council | She is going on a jungle holiday | Weeds only grow when it is cold
test: Mum yells "I'm not your bloody maid!" after finding socks on the stairs again. What would help most? | Put your own things away without being asked | Hire a maid with your pocket money | Hide the socks under the bed | Tell her she doesn't have to tidy
exam: Ascertain the consequence, pursuant to the regulations of the United Kingdom, of a motor vehicle failing its annual roadworthiness test. | It may not lawfully be driven until repaired | It shall be exempt from testing for one further year | It shall be confiscated and destroyed forthwith | Its owner shall receive a pecuniary reward
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a penalty notice affixed to a windscreen. | A fine which increases if not paid within the stipulated period | It constitutes a voucher redeemable at the adjacent supermarket | It constitutes an invitation to a road safety lesson | It constitutes a notice of no legal effect whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which purchasers frequently acquire a greater quantity of goods than intended within a supermarket. | The premises are designed to encourage additional purchases | Supermarkets are prohibited from selling small quantities | Shopping lists are invariably disregarded by law | Prices are lower for each additional item selected
exam: Whereas a vehicle has broken down upon a motorway, identify the course of action most consistent with safety. | The occupants shall leave the vehicle and wait behind the barrier | The occupants shall remain within the vehicle with doors secured | The occupants shall stand upon the carriageway to signal | The occupants shall proceed on foot along the hard shoulder to a garage
